### O005-D2 — delayed independent retrieval

Item version: v1. Role: full_capability_check. Relation: `parallel_variant`. Required components: R1, R2, R3, R4.

**Question:** Agent-created hypothetical: X=16, alpha=4, Y=5, Z=7, in common monetary units. State the one-shot sequence, role names, general feasible constraints and units, and numeric transfer bounds; derive general payoff, total and surplus formulas and calculate both payoffs, surplus, net investment gain and break-even return/proportion. Solve the pure-money continuation benchmark with its expectation assumption and explain why a maximum monetary sum need not improve both utilities. Retrieve the lecture’s approximate transfer percentages, show the conditional alpha=3 one-third/rounded .33 arithmetic and explain denominator/aggregation limits. Distinguish possible motives and interpret the supplied US/Korea/Vietnam rows, table columns, missing entry and absent methods. Supplied source-table excerpt (Studies / Sample / Average fraction sent / Average proportion returned): US 46 / 4552 / .51 / .34; South Korea 1 / 52 / .67 / .29; Vietnam 2 / 194 / .33 / n/a. Read the displayed averages descriptively and explain which population-trait, causal or significance claims these data do not establish; no memorization of study/sample counts is required.

**Key:** General constraints are X>0, alpha>1, 0≤Y≤X and 0≤Z≤alphaY; alpha is dimensionless and X/Y/Z use the same monetary units. The trustor/investor chooses Y first in [0,16]; the trustee/agent observes it and chooses Z in [0,20]. The interaction occurs once. Payoffs are X−Y+Z=18 and alphaY−Z=13; total is X+(alpha−1)Y=31; surplus is (alpha−1)Y=15; private gain is Z−Y=2. Break-even needs Z=Y=5; if the denominator is alphaY, the break-even fraction is 1/4. The trustee’s own payoff falls with Z, so Z=0 after every Y; a trustor expecting that continuation maximizes X−Y at Y=0. This requires both self-regarding preferences and corresponding expectations. Y=X maximizes total money under the stated cost-free multiplier rules, without guaranteeing either utility rises. The lecture reports approximately 45% of the endowment sent and 33% returned, with the return denominator and aggregation undefined. Conditional on Z/(3Y)=1/3, Z=Y and gain is zero; .33 instead gives Z=.99Y and gain=−.01Y, approximately zero. These calculations do not establish the actual denominator or reconstruct individual mean returns from separate averages. Repayment beliefs, altruism or efficiency can motivate Y; reciprocity may motivate Z but neither action identifies one motive. US: 46 studies, 4,552 observations, .51 sent/.34 returned; South Korea: 1 study, 52 observations, .67/.29; Vietnam: 2 studies, 194 observations, .33/n/a. Counts describe evidence quantity, transfer means describe choices, and n/a is missing rather than zero. Author/year, sampling/pooling/design controls, uncertainty and the return denominator are absent; the displayed averages can be ordered descriptively (Korea’s sent average exceeds the US value; the US return average exceeds Korea’s), but they do not establish causal country effects, representative rankings of national trust traits or statistical significance.

**Item rubric:** All listed required components must be supplied, with correct reasoning and limits; apply the component decision rules in Rubric and independence. Conceptually equivalent explanations and exact fractions/consistent decimals are acceptable. No assistance, notes, calculator, device, AI or key access for an independent/delayed attempt; blank paper and pen only. C1 is a supported repair after scoring the original response and does not count toward acquisition or delayed retention.

